# purpose: CI's `policy` check on every pull request: the branch kind decides what it may change (D21, `wsc policy`); a failure comments on the pull request (J42).
# usage: runs on GitHub Actions; locally: python3 core/wsc.py policy --branch <branch> --base origin/main
# when: automatic on pull requests; require the "policy" check in branch protection for main.
# not-a-verb: GitHub runs workflows by file; this one only calls `wsc policy`.
# Its own workflow so that gates.yml can run once per commit on push (J42): this one is cheap and needs the pull request's base and head.
# Draft pull requests skip it (J54): a job's builder opens its pull request as a draft when the job reaches review, so the review sees
# the branch's CI (the push-run checks attach to the commit and `wsc ci notify` comments on a red one) without a `policy` that must fail
# until the review says merge. The reviewer's `gh pr ready` (a merge verdict only, docs/REVIEW.md) sends ready_for_review, which runs it.
# J57: the reviewer marks the pull request ready BEFORE pushing the review commit (a skipped policy counts as passed, and `gh pr merge --auto`
# merges at once when the checks have passed), so the pushed head gets a real `policy` run. The ready_for_review run on the old head has no
# merge verdict and is red by design: the push cancels it (concurrency below), and `notify` stays silent for it.
name: policy
on:
  pull_request:
    types: [opened, synchronize, reopened, ready_for_review]
permissions:
  contents: read
concurrency:
  group: policy-${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
  cancel-in-progress: true
jobs:
{{wsc policy}}
{{wsc notify policy}}
